WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.720
THE STATE OF MICROSOFT:
Microsoft has given up on trying to innovate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:10.380
This is usually a terrible sign for companies.&nbsp;
We’ve seen countless examples of juggernauts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:16.500
being toppled over due to a lack of innovation&nbsp;
from Nokia and Xerox to Blockbuster and Yahoo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:23.820
But, this doesn’t seem to be hurting Microsoft, in&nbsp;
fact, at this point, they haven’t been innovating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:30.720
for quite some time. Microsoft is the only tech&nbsp;
giant that hasn’t created a revolutionary product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:37.560
within the past 10 to 20 years. Apple has the&nbsp;
iPhone, Google has Chrome, Amazon has AWS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:44.400
Tesla has electric self driving cars, and even&nbsp;
Meta is trying with the Metaverse but what about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:51.540
Microsoft? Microsoft is still really only known&nbsp;
for 2 products: Windows and Microsoft Office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:58.320
and it’s been nearly 40 years since Windows&nbsp;
as released and over 30 years since Office was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:04.200
released. For those of you commenting that they&nbsp;
created ChatGPT, they didn’t create that, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:09.960
bought that and this is really the secret behind&nbsp;
their success over the past 15 years. They’ve been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:15.900
trying to buy themselves to the top. Historically&nbsp;
speaking though, uninnovative companies buying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:21.780
innovative companies doesn’t usually work out.&nbsp;
Likely the most notable example of this is recent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:28.260
history is GE. Back in the late 2000s, they did&nbsp;
everything from jet engine manufacturing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:35.580
financing to producing appliances and pumping&nbsp;
gas. But, eventually, this became way too much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:43.380
to handle and GE would end up shrinking their&nbsp;
investments from $781 billion to $164 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:51.240
They’ve even spun off into 3 different companies&nbsp;
to try to lower the burden. So, little to say, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:57.780
can’t just buy yourself to the top. But, Microsoft&nbsp;
seems to be the exception as they’ve been doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:04.740
better than ever. In the past 5 years alone, their&nbsp;
revenue has doubled, their net income has tripled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:11.340
and their stock, well their stock has grown&nbsp;
10X within the past 10 years. For perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:18.240
that’s the same amount that Apple has grown and&nbsp;
they had the mobile revolution on their side. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:24.120
has Microsoft cracked the code to eternal&nbsp;
life or are they going to end up like GE?

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.240
FAUX INNOVATION:&nbsp;

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:38.940
Taking a look back, the reality is that Microsoft&nbsp;
has never been innovative with their products. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:43.980
key to their success has always been brilliant&nbsp;
business strategies and marketing as opposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:50.280
to actually creating the next big thing. This&nbsp;
is even true for Windows and Office. Windows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:56.940
was ripped off from the Mac which was ripped off&nbsp;
from Xerox. So, the product itself wasn’t all that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:02.880
innovative but what was innovative was Microsoft’s&nbsp;
go to market strategy. Instead of limiting their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:07.680
software to a specific piece of hardware, they&nbsp;
made it compatible with basically everything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.660
Not only did they make Windows compatible with&nbsp;
everything, they also made Windows accessible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:19.860
to everyone by actively encouraging piracy. And&nbsp;
if you don’t believe me, here’s Bill Gates on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:43.620
topic. As you can see, the key to Microsoft’s&nbsp;
early success was simply extraordinary market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:49.740
penetration. While not everyone was paying off&nbsp;
the bat, this strategy gave Microsoft unbelievable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:55.560
market control which they were eventually able to&nbsp;
monetize by licencing to PC makers, corporations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:02.100
universities, and governments. This was really&nbsp;
phase 1 of Microsoft’s rise, they were able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:07.860
to successfully substitute market penetration&nbsp;
for innovation. Moving onto phase 2, we would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:13.200
see Microsoft substitute market domination for&nbsp;
innovation. The first notable example of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:19.380
was Internet Explorer. Microsoft saw the explosive&nbsp;
success of the internet back in the early 90s with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:26.820
AOL and Netscape, and they decided they wanted a&nbsp;
piece of the pie. So, in 1995, Microsoft would go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:34.980
ahead and launch IE. But again, there was nothing&nbsp;
particularly special about IE. It was just a run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:42.600
of the mill browser with even less inspiring name.&nbsp;
I mean, it was literally named Internet Explorer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:48.000
At least, there’s no confusion about what it&nbsp;
does I guess. But, none of this mattered because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:53.760
Microsoft once again had an effective strategy&nbsp;
to get the product to people. The strategy was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:59.820
to simply bundle in IE with Windows. Sure, people&nbsp;
could go out and download whatever browser they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:05.880
want after they buy the computer. But, back in the&nbsp;
late 1990s, people were just then getting their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:12.180
first computers, so most people had no baseline&nbsp;
or prior expectations. Everything was new to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:18.420
them so might as well stick with the browser&nbsp;
that’s already there. This would lead to IE’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:25.680
marketshare rising to 95% by 2003 and a slew of&nbsp;
antitrust lawsuits but that’s a wholenother story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:32.040
The takeaway here is that IE wasn’t successful&nbsp;
because it was innovative. IE was successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:37.740
because Microsoft was able to leverage their&nbsp;
domination with Windows. Microsoft would use this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:43.140
same strategy on a few more products throughout&nbsp;
the late 90s and early 2000s. MSN Search,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:48.600
for example, was decently popular because it&nbsp;
was pushed by IE which was pushed by Windows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:54.240
The same thing could be said about Hotmail and&nbsp;
MSN Messenger and basically all of Microsoft’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:58.980
other products during this time period. But,&nbsp;
if you read the news at the time, you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:04.980
see headlines that yes Microsoft was a monopoly&nbsp;
but also that they were the most innovative tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:10.200
company in the world. And let’s just say,&nbsp;
Microsoft would let this go to their head.

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:14.040
HOPE IS LOST:&nbsp;

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:25.560
By the end of the 1900s, Microsoft had become the&nbsp;
world’s largest company and they were synonymous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:32.820
with tech. Fun fact, Microsoft is the only top 10&nbsp;
company from the year 2000 that’s still a top 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:39.780
company today. But maintaining this level&nbsp;
of success was by no means easy. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:46.020
the next 10 years would be the decade of failure&nbsp;
for Microsoft as they would try to innovate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:51.480
their inability to innovate would really shine&nbsp;
through. The first notable example of this came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:58.080
in 2006 with the Microsoft Zune. Most of you have&nbsp;
probably never even heard about the Microsoft Zune&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:03.540
and the main reason for this was that it was&nbsp;
such a massive flop. The Microsoft Zune was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:08.640
essentially Microsoft’s answer to the iPod, but&nbsp;
there were a lot of fundamental issues with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:15.540
Zune starting with timing. For starters, the Zune&nbsp;
came out 5 years after the original iPod meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:21.780
that the iPod was already the go to product for&nbsp;
most people. So, Microsoft was fighting an uphill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:27.840
battle from day 1, but that doesn't necessarily&nbsp;
have to be a bad thing. Plenty of successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:33.360
products came out super late like Chrome,&nbsp;
Gmail, and Zoom. But the reason that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:37.920
products were able to takeover was because they&nbsp;
were categorically better than the competition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:44.760
The same could not be said about the Zune. The&nbsp;
Zune was by no means a bad product. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:51.660
users liked it just as much as the iPod and it&nbsp;
costed the same as the iPod. But, this was also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:58.200
the biggest problem. If its identical to the iPod,&nbsp;
who would buy it over the iPod which was already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:04.500
the popular thing? Well, the answer is no one and&nbsp;
Microsoft would cancel the Zune just a few years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:09.360
later. Microsoft should’ve learned from this and&nbsp;
approached smartphones with a different strategy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:15.960
but Microsoft would go ahead and make a fool of&nbsp;
themselves not much later. I mean just listen to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:36.180
former Microsoft CEO’s reaction to the reveal of&nbsp;
the iPhone. Now, to his credit, he did clarify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:42.060
that it might sell really well, but if that’s&nbsp;
the case, you probably shouldn’t be laughing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.060 --> 00:09:02.760
at it. Instead, you should be looking at why it&nbsp;
might sell well, who will be buying the product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:09.300
and how you can get a piece of the pie. Microsoft&nbsp;
was in the perfect position to become the Windows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:14.940
of the smartphone world, but instead they would&nbsp;
let Google take this spot. In the meantime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:21.900
Microsoft would sit around for 3 years and come&nbsp;
out with the Windows Phone in 2010. The worst part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:28.740
though was the OS. The reason the iPhone was&nbsp;
so successful was not because it was a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:35.460
computer but because it was a reimagined small&nbsp;
computer. Every single application, interaction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:42.540
and were reimagined to work on a smaller screen&nbsp;
with fingers instead of a mouse. You could say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:49.140
that Apple was indeed innovating. With the Windows&nbsp;
phone on the other hand, you basically got a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:55.500
beta version of Windows 8 on a smaller screen.&nbsp;
Unsurprisingly, the Windows Phone would not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.500 --> 00:10:02.880
be a humiliating failure but also an expensive&nbsp;
one. But unlike with the Zune, Microsoft would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:08.880
actually learn from the Windows phone, and let’s&nbsp;
just say, they’ve turned things around big time.

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:18.000
ACQUISTIONS GALORE:&nbsp;

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:24.300
Usually, companies have quite an ego when it comes&nbsp;
to acknowledging or acquiring a competitor. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:29.640
usually want to do everything themselves. This&nbsp;
was very much the case with Microsoft as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:35.040
Instead of working with AOL and Netscape,&nbsp;
Microsoft decided to compete against them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:41.280
Instead of working with the iPod and iPhone,&nbsp;
Microsoft decided to compete against Apple. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:46.920
all of this would change when Satya Nadella&nbsp;
tookover the company in early 2014. Satya&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:53.580
was very different from his peer CEOs. Most tech&nbsp;
CEOs are world class in specific fields. Tim Cook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:59.820
for example, is world class at supply chain&nbsp;
and operations. Similarly, Sundar Pichai is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:06.000
the technical genius behind Chrome. Satya, on the&nbsp;
other hand, wasn’t an expert in any one field nor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:12.600
did he go to IIT in India or Stanford and Harvard.&nbsp;
This isn’t to say that Satya was incompetent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:18.900
but the biggest characteristic he brought to&nbsp;
the table was not a technical skill. Rather, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:24.360
was humility. He had the humility to accept that&nbsp;
younger newer companies were simply doing things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:30.660
better, and instead of trying to resist like his&nbsp;
predecessors, he was going to embrace leading to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:37.560
Microsoft’s era of acquisitions. He started of&nbsp;
relatively small with Mojang in 2014 for $2.5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:43.080
billion. These are the guys behind Minecraft.&nbsp;
But, it didn’t take him long to scale up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:51.240
In 2016, Microsoft would go ahead and buy LinkedIn&nbsp;
for $26.2 billion. He followed this up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:59.760
GitHub for $7.5 billion, ZeniMax Media for $7.5&nbsp;
billion, Nuance Communications for $19.7 billion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:07.020
Activision Blizzard for $68.7 billion, and of&nbsp;
course $10 billion into ChatGPT. Those are just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:12.360
a few of Microsoft’s most notable investments&nbsp;
and acquisitions. Here’s a full list of all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:18.840
Microsoft’s known purchases. This is stark&nbsp;
contrast to every other tech giant. Apple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:25.680
for example, just avoids high profile acquisitions&nbsp;
altogether. Their last big purchase was Beats and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:31.080
that was almost 10 years ago. Google on the&nbsp;
other hand does do a crap ton of acquisitions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:36.060
but almost all of them are in the tens of&nbsp;
millions or hundreds of millions. They’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:41.460
not in the billions or tens of billions. They&nbsp;
clearly prefer to play an active role in building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:46.920
up brands as opposed to buying out established&nbsp;
businesses. The only other tech company that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:51.720
has engaged in such massive deals is Facebook,&nbsp;
but there’s a key difference between Facebook’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:58.020
purchases and Microsoft’s purchases. Facebook buys&nbsp;
products to integrate into their own ecosystem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:03.540
Microsoft buys companies as passive investments&nbsp;
and keeps them as separate from the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:10.740
business as possible. If you don’t believe me,&nbsp;
check this out. It’s been 7 years since Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:16.800
acquired LinkedIn, but to this day, there is&nbsp;
no sign in with Microsoft or Outlook option,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:23.460
though there is a sign in with Google or Apple&nbsp;
option. The same thing can be said about GitHub.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:28.860
To create a GitHub account, you have to do it the&nbsp;
old fashioned way by entering an email address,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:35.520
you can’t just login with Microsoft. It’s not&nbsp;
just signing in either. Users of these services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:40.620
wouldn’t even know that these companies are owned&nbsp;
by Microsoft unless they were explicitly told.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:46.500
This goes much deeper than just superficial&nbsp;
things as well. If you ask the CEOs of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:52.080
acquired companies, they have nothing but good&nbsp;
things to say about Microsoft. “Microsoft has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:57.240
not forgotten why we did the deal in the first&nbsp;
place and what the important pillars of the deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.240 --> 00:14:03.060
are. The first and foremost principle is&nbsp;
to put developers first. And that is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:08.580
we do every day.” In stark contrast, all&nbsp;
of WhatsApp and Instagram’s founders have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:14.640
left Facebook on negative terms. Clearly,&nbsp;
Microsoft is not just acquiring a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:19.020
companies for a bunch of money, they’re&nbsp;
also maintaining them in the right way.

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:21.900
A HOLDING COMPANY:&nbsp;

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:32.340
In the beginning of the video, we talked about&nbsp;
how Microsoft is the only tech giant that hasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.340 --> 00:14:38.280
created a revolutionary product in the past&nbsp;
10 to 20 years, but they’re also the only tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:46.800
giant that isn’t dependent on one revolutionary&nbsp;
product. To this day, over 50% of all of Google’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:53.220
revenue comes from Google Search. Over 50% of&nbsp;
Apple’s revenue comes from the iPhone. The only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:59.220
reason Amazon is even profitable is because&nbsp;
of AWS. And the entire business of Meta can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:05.340
be summed up by Facebook, WhatsApp, and Instagram.&nbsp;
Microsoft on the other hand isn’t tied down by one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:12.540
or even a handful of products. They are truly a&nbsp;
diversified business with hundreds and hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:19.140
products each operating on independent terms.&nbsp;
Before, the sentiment around Microsoft was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.140 --> 00:15:25.320
they were able to transition from being a consumer&nbsp;
business to being an enterprise business. But this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:30.060
doesn’t paint the full story as they have since&nbsp;
transitioned from being an enterprise business to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:36.720
being a full on holding company, and that is the&nbsp;
state of Microsoft today and why they’re able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:42.360
get away without innovating themselves. Are you&nbsp;
a fan of the new Microsoft? Comment that down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:47.580
below. Also, drop a like if you would like to&nbsp;
see more videos about the evolution of various&nbsp;&nbsp;

